terAttachmentName":"Attachment D - PC Memo - Z-25-26-1-2.pdf","MatterAttachmentHyperlink":"https://phoenix.legistar1.com/phoenix/attachments/0f32afff-4f91-44bf-9bba-a3fb0ead858e.pdf","MatterAttachmentFileName":"0f32afff-4f91-44bf-9bba-a3fb0ead858e.pdf","MatterAttachmentMatterVersion":"0","MatterAttachmentIsHyperlink":false,"MatterAttachmentBinary":null,"MatterAttachmentIsSupportingDocument":false,"MatterAttachmentShowOnInternetPage":true,"MatterAttachmentIsMinuteOrder":false,"MatterAttachmentIsBoardLetter":false,"MatterAttachmentAgiloftId":0,"MatterAttachmentDescription":null,"MatterAttachmentPrintWithReports":false,"MatterAttachmentSort":-2147483648},{"MatterAttachmentId":73767,"MatterAttachmentGuid":"8A40462F-7F0B-4041-B3C7-926520AA544E","MatterAttachmentLastModifiedUtc":"2026-06-09T20:02:32.453","MatterAttachmentRowVersion":"AAAAAASMBLE=","MatterAttachmentName":"Attachment F - Support - Z-25-26-1-2.pdf","MatterAttachmentHyperlink":"https://phoenix.legistar1.com/phoenix/attachments/876f018b-2ba9-4df6-92cc-f5fb2625b938.pdf","MatterAttachmentFileName":"876f018b-2ba9-4df6-92cc-f5fb2625b938.pdf","MatterAttachmentMatterVersion":"0","MatterAttachmentIsHyperlink":false,"MatterAttachmentBinary":null,"MatterAttachmentIsSupportingDocument":false,"MatterAttachmentShowOnInternetPage":true,"MatterAttachmentIsMinuteOrder":false,"MatterAttachmentIsBoardLetter":false,"MatterAttachmentAgiloftId":0,"MatterAttachmentDescription":null,"MatterAttachmentPrintWithReports":false,"MatterAttachmentSort":-2147483648},{"MatterAttachmentId":73912,"MatterAttachmentGuid":"BFAD3F9B-FBF4-4B72-B39D-43905241EA9C","MatterAttachmentLastModifiedUtc":"2026-06-24T17:47:19.513","MatterAttachmentRowVersion":"AAAAAASMBNU=","MatterAttachmentName":"Attachment A - Draft Ordinance - Z-25-26-1-2.pdf","MatterAttachmentHyperlink":"https://phoenix.legistar1.com/phoenix/attachments/07040ab1-7d4d-45c4-8247-68406b5115f2.pdf","MatterAttachmentFileName":"07040ab1-7d4d-45c4-8247-68406b5115f2.pdf","MatterAttachmentMatterVersion":"0","MatterAttachmentIsHyperlink":false,"MatterAttachmentBinary":null,"MatterAttachmentIsSupportingDocument":false,"MatterAttachmentShowOnInternetPage":true,"MatterAttachmentIsMinuteOrder":false,"MatterAttachmentIsBoardLetter":false,"MatterAttachmentAgiloftId":0,"MatterAttachmentDescription":null,"MatterAttachmentPrintWithReports":false,"MatterAttachmentSort":-2147483648}]},{"EventItemId":64858,"EventItemGuid":"786598C1-3F25-42DB-A512-BDC46EE048AD","EventItemLastModifiedUtc":"2026-08-04T17:01:28.943","EventItemRowVersion":"AAAAAASVFl8=","EventItemEventId":2651,"EventItemAgendaSequence":102,"EventItemMinutesSequence":104,"EventItemAgendaNumber":"90","EventItemVideo":null,"EventItemVideoIndex":null,"EventItemVersion":"1","EventItemAgendaNote":null,"EventItemMinutesNote":"{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\deff0\\deflang1033{\\fonttbl{\\f0\\fnil\\fcharset0 Arial;}}\r\n{\\colortbl ;\\red0\\green0\\blue0;}\r\n\\viewkind4\\uc1\\pard\\cf1\\b\\fs28 Discussion\\par\r\n\\b0 Councilman Waring suggested the Council hear from the residents petitioning against the item first.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMayor Gallego agreed and declared the public hearing open.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nDoug Miller expressed his concern regarding the proposed roadway connection at Ranger Drive. He explained that Ranger Drive is currently the only access point to his home from Tatum Boulevard and described the original plan for the road to connect to 56th Street as part of the Desert Ridge Specific Plan and the street classification map for the last couple decades. He stated this connection was consistent with the approved traffic impact analysis and that construction costs were originally expected to be covered by the developer. He noted that the new addendum proposed a temporary cul-de-sac instead of completing the connection, which shifts financial responsibility to a future City capital improvement project. He reviewed existing and projected traffic conditions in the area, citing data showed that Ranger Drive\\rquote s current traffic levels were comparable to similar neighborhood connector roads. He argued that opening the road would not create a major cut through. He concluded that the connection had long been planned, is included in the general plan, and should be completed by the developer rather than put upon the taxpayers.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMayor Gallego asked if Councilman Waring wanted to hear from the applicant or would rather first hear from the other registered speakers.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring answered the registered speakers should go first.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nBarry Goldfarb expressed his opposition to this item.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nBrandy Staab expressed her support for this item.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nAlbert Carrino expressed his support for this item.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nClaudia Garza expressed her support for this item.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nBrian Olson expressed his support for this item.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nNeal Peterson expressed his support for this item.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nNick Wood, representative for the applicant, introduced himself.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring stated when Mr. Wood informed him the proposal had failed at the village planning committee earlier in the year, at a time when the roadway connection was still part of the application, he was disappointed because the community had supported the project under initial efforts. He noted the only issue causing the failure was the roadway requirement from the City, and that in his view the project itself had not changed between the first and second votes except for the road.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMr. Wood confirmed the proposal failed at the village.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring stated he generally does not support projects that fail at the village planning committee and relies on those recommendations. He noted the Desert Ridge HOA board president, who serves on the committee and has a long-standing working relationship with, voted against the initial proposal. He explained that after the first vote failed due to the roadway component they worked to identify an acceptable alternative. He stated the revised version, which removed the road, passed 9-0 at the village and asked if that was correct.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMr. Wood confirmed his statement and added the revised proposal was passed by the Planning Commission as well.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring stated his understanding was the Planning Commission supported the revised proposal unanimously, noting that about 15 or 16 volunteer members voted in favor, whereas the initial proposal had failed. He referenced comments indicating that residents had signed a petition opposing the roadway and said his office heard from significantly more residents against the road than in support of it. He added this aligned with attendance and feedback at community meetings held by the applicant.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMr. Wood confirmed that many people attended the first village planning committee hearing and the second.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring said he is familiar with the Desert Ridge area from years of door knocking. He emphasized the planned road connection had long been part of the area\\rquote s plans but felt it did not make sense to let it prevent the project from moving forward, especially as Desert Ridge continues to attract new residents. He remarked the Desert Ridge resort is a major asset to District 2 and that its strong opposition to the roadway was significant. He also referenced concerns raised by long-time residents and former village planning committee members, noting that most feedback opposed the initial proposal of the road while supporting the revised proposal. He emphasized his long-working relationship with the Desert Ridge HOA board and praised their commitment to the community. He asked for confirmation that the revised plan includes a park the applicant would maintain.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMr. Wood confirmed that statement.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring emphasized the applicant was not avoiding costs but reallocating resources under the revised plan. He expressed appreciation for the applicant\\rquote s willingness to name the planned park after Doug Dixon, the long-time Desert Ridge HOA president, noting Mr. Dixon\\rquote s contributions to the community. Councilman Waring noted the initial project failed at the village because of the roadway issue, not because of concerns about the project itself, and said that the road option was no longer viable given resident opposition.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring said he intended to move forward with the motion, but noted that some residents remained concerned about future road construction. He referenced comments made by Mr. Miller regarding funding responsibility for the roadway and stated the road did not appear in the City\\rquote s five year capital improvement plan (CIP). He asked Planning and Development Director Joshua Bednarek to expand on that topic.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMr. Bednarek explained that because the roadway was not included in the City\\rquote s current five-year capital improvement plan, the Council would need to identify and approve funding for it in a future CIP budget. He conveyed the project could only move forward if such funding was designated by the Council.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring stated because the roadway was not included in the current five-year capital improvement plan, the earliest it could be considered for funding would be Fiscal Year 2031.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMr. Bednarek stated determining the next possible time frame for funding the roadway would require input from the Budget and Research Director, the City Manager\\rquote s Office, or the Street Transportation Department.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nCouncilman Waring stated any future construction of the roadway would require a vote of a future Council to add funding to a later capital improvement plan, noting that no funding currently exists and he will be term limited by the time such a decision could be made. He reiterated that shifting the cost from the developer to the City only becomes relevant if the road is ever built, which he does not anticipate given long-standing neighborhood opposition and the hotel\\rquote s opposition. He voiced his intent to support the item and expressed appreciation to the developer for working with him and considering community feedback. He asked if he had missed any key points.\\par\r\n\\par\r\nMr. Bednarek confirmed Councilman Waring\\rquote s description of the process and reiterated the stipulation requires the developer provide and dedicate the necessary right-of-way for the roadway. Mr. Bednarek noted that some portions are already paved, so land acquisition is not an issue. He clarified that any future discussion would relate solely to funding for road construction, which would need to be considered by a future Council.\\par\r\n\\b\\par\r\nMotion was made by Councilman Waring motioned to approve this item and adopt the related ordinance.\\b0\\par\r\n\\par\r\nNoting there was no one else present wishing to speak, Mayor Gallego declared the public hearing closed.\\par\r\n\\par\r\n\\b The motion was seconded by Councilwoman Pastor.\\par\r\n}\r\n","EventItemActionId":363,"EventItemActionName":"adopted","EventItemActionText":"The hearing was held. A motion was made by Councilman Waring, seconded by Councilwoman Pastor, that this item be approved per the Planning Commission recommendation with adoption of the related ordinance. The motion carried by the following vote:","EventItemPassedFlag":1,"EventItemPassedFlagName":"Pass","EventItemRollCallFlag":0,"EventItemFlagExtra":0,"EventItemTitle":"Public Hearing and Ordinance Adoption - Rezoning Application Z-15-26-2 - Southwest Corner of 56th Street and Ranger Drive (Ordinance G-7525) - District 2","EventItemTally":null,"EventItemAccelaRecordId":null,"EventItemConsent":0,"EventItemMoverId":192,"EventItemMover":"Jim Waring","EventItemSeconderId":195,"EventItemSeconder":"Laura Pastor","EventItemMatter